For this project, I created a cloud-hosted web server using Microsoft Azure. I used an Azure for Students subscription, which provided initial free credits to create and test the server.
The server was created using Infrastructure as a Service because I had direct SSH access to the virtual machine and manually configured the server environment myself. 
I configured the required network ports, installed and used Nginx, deployed a custom HTML website, connected a Namecheap domain name, and configured HTTPS/SSL.
The website is called Classic Cars Showcase. It is a static HTML website that displays information about selected classic cars.


This project uses Infrastructure as a Service because I created and managed my own cloud virtual machine instead of using a pre-built hosting platform.
I was responsible for:
Creating the Azure virtual machine
Connecting to the server using SSH
Configuring network ports
Managing the Linux command-line environment
Installing and configuring Nginx
Uploading and editing website files
Connecting a custom domain name
Configuring HTTPS/SSL
Testing and troubleshooting the server
This gave me direct control over the operating system, server software and network configuration.


The project started by creating a virtual machine in Microsoft Azure. I used the Azure for Students subscription because it provided free initial credits for learning and testing.
Ubuntu Linux was selected as the operating system because it is commonly used for web servers and can be managed through the command line.
After creating the virtual machine, I configured the required inbound network ports.
Port	Service	Purpose

22	SSH	allows remote command-line access to the server

80	HTTP	allows normal website traffic

443	HTTPS	allows secure website traffic

These ports were required so that I could manage the server remotely and allow users to access the website through a browser.

